Natalia

Thanks to the fast coding of Dave Miller, the bug that prevented your fitting of a hazard rate key function model to your binned data has been resolved.

The trouble resided in the mrds package, so Dave has patched mrds.  To install this version of mrds, issue these commands:

library(devtools)
install_github("DistanceDevelopment/mrds")

You can then proceed to install the Distance package you already have and run the ds() function in this manner

hr.5pct.bins <- ds(data=natalia, key="hr", truncation = 719,
                   cutpoints = seq(from=0, to=719, length.out = 7))

to produce

In my opinion, you need to understand why you have the bump in your detections between 500 and 600m.  This hazard rate model with cosine adjustments is trying to accommodate that bump--is it noise in the data, or a process that causes this?  You can judge whether it might be preferable to smooth through that bump with a half normal model.

