I actually have code somewhere that does this. The best solution is to use angle bins which don't change and then you can specify the detection fct in terms of angle.  See attached.

The std formula for density becomes

where a subscript is for angle and H is height over ground. With H varying this just turns into a H-T type estimator using H_i (height for the ith observation).

Not sure if it is supported in DISTANCE or Distance R package but I set up mrds to be able to handle this by having a dist.begin and dist.end and inner and outer widths applied to inner band (if not line) and outer band because that width changes with altitude.

--jeff

